  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how biomining and biorefining can be integrated into metal recovery from ore. The top schematic shows a conventional mining flow—excavation, crushing, and grinding—followed by several possible routes to obtain dissolved or concentrated metal. Instead of relying only on extraction after fine grinding, the diagram highlights biological alternatives that can act at different stages: in situ bioleaching directly in the deposit, heap bioleaching on crushed ore, and biooxidation after grinding. These routes feed into a liquid product that is then further treated.\n\nThe lower part emphasizes two linked ideas. “Bioleaching” is illustrated in three forms: heap treatment of piled ore, in situ treatment through fluid injection into rock, and biooxidation in a vessel, all suggesting microbes help mobilize or expose metals. “Biorefinement” then describes how metals are recovered from solution using biological processes, including biosorption or bioaccumulation, and bioprecipitation or bioreduction. Arrows connect purification and biorefinement to a final refinement step that produces metal bars, implying a full pathway from ore to purified metal with biological processing reducing or replacing some conventional extraction steps.",
